    <p>Ask ten process engineers to explain the difference between a sight glass and a sight flow indicator and you will get ten slightly different answers — which is exactly why the two products get mixed up in specifications. Both are transparent, pressure-rated windows that let you see into a process without opening the system, and at a glance they look similar. But they answer different questions. A sight glass tells you <strong>what is inside</strong> the vessel or line: level, color, clarity. A sight flow indicator tells you <strong>whether the media is actually moving</strong>, in which direction, and at roughly what rate. This guide defines both products, compares them across function, indication method, installation, price and maintenance, walks through when to choose each, and ends with a decision checklist you can hand to a specifier today.</p>

    <h2 id="what-is-a-sight-glass">What Is a Sight Glass?</h2>
    <p>A sight glass — also called a sight window, inspection glass or observation port — is a pressure-rated transparent assembly fitted into a vessel wall or into a pipeline so operators can observe the process media without opening the system. It is a passive component with three parts: a metal body, a glass window rated to the process pressure and temperature, and a gasket that seals the window in place. Nothing inside moves.</p>
    <p>Sight glasses fall into two families. <strong>Vessel sight glasses</strong> are mounted on tanks, reactors and storage vessels, where their job is to show liquid level, product color, mixing behavior, foaming or sediment build-up. <strong>Pipeline sight glasses</strong> are installed inline in a pipe, where their job is to show that media is present and what it looks like as it passes the window. In the SIGHTSPEC range, vessel and pipeline observation is covered by the <a href="/products/tri-clamp-sight-glasses/">SG-100 tri-clamp sight glass</a> for sanitary duty, the <a href="/products/threaded-sight-glasses/">SG-300 threaded sight glass</a> for compact high-pressure lines up to 100 bar, and the <a href="/products/flanged-sight-glasses/">SG-400 flanged sight glass</a> for large windows on chemical and oil &amp; gas lines.</p>
    <p>The limitation of a plain sight glass is that it cannot, by itself, tell you whether the media is moving. A pipe full of static liquid looks the same through a window as a pipe with liquid flowing past it — the operator sees the same glass, the same liquid, the same color. That gap is exactly what the sight flow indicator was designed to fill.</p>

    <h2 id="what-is-a-sight-flow-indicator">What Is a Sight Flow Indicator?</h2>
    <p>A sight flow indicator is an inline viewing device that combines a transparent window with an internal moving element that responds to flow. That moving element is the defining difference. It gives positive, visual proof that media is moving, which direction it is moving, and — with some element types — a rough sense of rate.</p>
    <p>Four element designs cover the majority of applications. A <strong>flapper</strong> (or paddle) is hinged at the top of the body and pushed open by the flow; if the line stops, the flapper drops, and the change is obvious. A <strong>rotary</strong> element uses vanes or a rotor that spins at a rate roughly proportional to flow, making it the best choice when relative flow rate matters. A <strong>drip tube</strong> is used on gravity and vent lines: liquid drips through a visible tube, and the drip rate is the indication. A <strong>ball</strong> element is lifted or rolled by the flow and settles when flow stops. Each element is selected for the media, the line orientation and the question the operator needs answered.</p>
    <p>The SIGHTSPEC <a href="/products/sight-flow-indicators/">SG-200 sight flow indicator</a> covers tri-clamp, threaded, flanged and weld mounting, with borosilicate glass for high-temperature duty and soda lime for low-temperature service, in 304 / 316L stainless steel or carbon steel. Depending on element and body, ratings reach up to 500°F and 300 psi, and the range splits into a hygienic line and an industrial line so the same basic product serves both pharmaceutical and heavy-duty process duty.</p>

    <h2 id="comparison-table">Sight Glass vs Sight Flow Indicator: Comparison Table</h2>
    <table>
