      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Both products share the same glass reality: the window is the pressure boundary and the weakest link, so it must be rated for the worst-case pressure and temperature combination, and it must be treated as a consumable. For a deeper look at window materials, see our <a href="/blog/sight-glass-glass-types/">borosilicate vs soda lime vs quartz comparison</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="when-to-use-a-sight-glass">When a Sight Glass Is the Right Choice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Level and inventory checks on vessels</strong> — tanks, reactors and storage vessels where the operator needs to see how full the vessel is. Large windows such as the SG-400 flanged design suit this duty.</li>
      <li><strong>Batch quality and product changeover</strong> — color, clarity and contamination checks on <a href="/applications/pharmaceutical/">pharmaceutical</a>, <a href="/applications/food-beverage/">food and beverage</a> and <a href="/applications/chemical-processing/">chemical</a> batches without opening the system.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixing and agitation observation</strong> — confirming that agitators are actually mixing, that powders are wetting out, or that foam is forming where it should not.</li>
      <li><strong>Sanitary vessels</strong> — the SG-100 tri-clamp sight glass with USP Class VI / FDA-compliant assembly is built for hygienic tanks where dead spaces are unacceptable.</li>
      <li><strong>Simple, low-cost inline observation</strong> — when the only question is "is there media in this line and what does it look like," a plain pipeline sight glass is the economical answer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Choose a sight glass when the operator's question is about the <em>state of the media</em>: level, appearance, condition. It shows you the process; it does not measure it.</p>

    <h2 id="when-to-use-a-flow-indicator">When a Sight Flow Indicator Is the Right Choice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pump and circulation verification</strong> — seal water, cooling water and recirculation lines where a stopped pump must be spotted immediately. A flapper or rotary element makes a dead line unmistakable.</li>
      <li><strong>Lubrication and oil return lines</strong> — confirming that oil is actually returning to the reservoir, not just that the reservoir has oil in it.</li>
      <li><strong>Purge gas, vent and drain lines</strong> — gravity and low-pressure lines where a drip tube shows drip rate at a glance.</li>
      <li><strong>CIP / SIP flow verification</strong> — confirming cleaning media actually passes through the line during cleaning cycles, not just that the cycle timer ran.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear or invisible media</strong> — transparent liquids and gases that look identical whether static or moving; the moving element is the only visual proof of flow.</li>
      <li><strong>Dead-leg and blockage detection</strong> — on <a href="/applications/oil-gas/">oil and gas</a> and process lines where a blocked or air-locked section must be found quickly.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Choose a sight flow indicator when the operator's question is about <em>movement</em>: is flow happening, in which direction, and at roughly what rate?</p>

    <h2 id="how-to-choose">How to Choose: A Decision Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1. What question must the operator answer?</strong> "What is in the vessel / what does the media look like?" → sight glass. "Is the media moving?" → sight flow indicator.</li>
      <li><strong>2. Where does it install?</strong> Vessel wall, tank or reactor → sight glass family. Inline in a pipe → either; add the flow question to decide.</li>
      <li><strong>3. Is flow confirmation part of the job?</strong> Pump verification, cooling, seal water, drain or purge lines → sight flow indicator. If flow confirmation matters, a plain window will disappoint.</li>
      <li><strong>4. Do you need direction or rate?</strong> Rough rate or direction → rotary element. On/off flow only → flapper or ball. Gravity drip rate → drip tube.</li>
      <li><strong>5. Pressure and temperature?</strong> High pressure up to 100 bar → SG-300 threaded. Up to 300 psi and 500°F → SG-200 flow indicator or SG-100 tri-clamp. Large flanged lines → SG-400, ANSI Class 150/300.</li>
      <li><strong>6. Hygienic or pharmaceutical duty?</strong> Use tri-clamp versions with USP Class VI / FDA-compliant glass, gaskets and surface finish — the SG-100 and the hygienic SG-200 line.</li>
      <li><strong>7. Budget and maintenance capacity?</strong> Sight glasses cost less and have no moving parts; flow indicators cost slightly more and add one wear point to inspect.</li>
      <li><strong>8. Still unsure?</strong> Send your media, line size, connection, pressure and temperature to our <a href="/contact/">engineers</a>, or work through our <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-a-sight-glass/">6-question selection guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>One rule applies to both: specify the worst-case combination — maximum pressure together with maximum temperature, plus vacuum if it can occur — and verify the glass type against the media, because the window is the pressure boundary.</p>

    <h2 id="can-you-use-both">Can You Use a Sight Glass and a Sight Flow Indicator Together?</h2>
    <p>Yes — and in practice they are frequently installed on the same skid, because they answer different questions. A common arrangement is a vessel with a sight glass for level, plus a flow indicator on the discharge line to verify the pump is actually moving product. Another is a plain sight glass upstream to watch media condition and a flow indicator downstream to confirm flow reaches the next unit. They do not replace each other: a flow indicator tells you nothing about level, and a vessel sight glass tells you nothing about movement.</p>
    <p>Two practical notes when combining them. First, rate each window independently — the pressure and temperature at a vessel nozzle can differ from the conditions at a discharge line. Second, treat both windows as consumables and put them on the same inspection schedule; checking for cracks, chips and clouding is the most effective sight glass maintenance practice there is.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Sight Glass vs Sight Flow Indicator FAQ</h2>
    <details><summary>Is a sight flow indicator just a sight glass with a moving part?</summary><p>Essentially yes. The body, glass and gasket are the same family of components, and the moving element (flapper, rotary, drip tube or ball) is added to give positive visual proof of flow. That element is also the main reason a flow indicator costs a little more and needs one extra inspection point.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can a plain sight glass show whether flow is happening?</summary><p>Only indirectly. Through a plain window, static liquid and flowing liquid can look identical, especially with clear media. If confirming flow matters to your operation, use a sight flow indicator — the moving element makes a stopped line obvious.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which one is more expensive, and why?</summary><p>A sight flow indicator generally costs more than an equivalent sight glass. The extra cost comes from the moving element and the more complex body that houses it. Sight glasses are simpler and cheaper when observation of media condition is all you need.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can both be used in hygienic and pharmaceutical lines?</summary><p>Yes, in tri-clamp versions. The SG-100 sight glass and the hygienic SG-200 flow indicator line use sanitary tri-clamp mounting with USP Class VI / FDA-compliant glass and gaskets, so both are suitable for pharmaceutical and food duty where dead spaces are not acceptable.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I replace an existing sight glass with a sight flow indicator?</summary><p>Often yes, if the mounting and ratings match — same connection type and size, and pressure/temperature ratings equal to or better than the line conditions. Confirm the glass type suits the media, and check line orientation, because some elements suit vertical lines and others horizontal. When in doubt, send the existing installation details to our engineers.</p></details>
